From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim Besard <tim.besard@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 22:14:14 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Drop tune-cpu when unifying function attributes

Since LLVM 23, clang defaults to -mtune=apple-m5 on macOS (llvm#179136),
so a kernel library built for Apple silicon carries "tune-cpu"="apple-m5",
while kernels from SPIR-V or the in-process front-end have no tune-cpu.
AArch64's areInlineCompatible compares subtarget feature bits, which
include the tuned processor family (the ARMProcFamily features are not
InlineIgnore), so the inliner rejects every call between the two kinds of
functions, even for alwaysinline callees.

The barrier helpers (_cl_work_group_barrier etc.) then stay out-of-line
calls. The work-group passes do not see a barrier in the kernel, the
work-item loop spans the whole kernel, and local memory written before the
barrier is read before the other work-items wrote it.

unifyLLVMFunctionAttributes already unifies target-cpu and target-features
for this reason; drop tune-cpu as well, so all functions tune for the
target CPU.

Assisted-by: Claude Code (Opus 5.5)
---
 lib/llvmopencl/LLVMUtils.cc | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/lib/llvmopencl/LLVMUtils.cc b/lib/llvmopencl/LLVMUtils.cc
index ec52bddbc..50b8006e8 100644
--- a/lib/llvmopencl/LLVMUtils.cc
+++ b/lib/llvmopencl/LLVMUtils.cc
@@ -933,6 +933,14 @@ void unifyLLVMFunctionAttributes(llvm::Module &M, bool TouchAlwaysInline) {
       F.addFnAttr("target-cpu", TargetCPU);
     if (!TargetFeatures.empty())
       F.addFnAttr("target-features", TargetFeatures);
+    // Tune for the target CPU everywhere. Clang 23 defaults to
+    // -mtune=apple-m5 on macOS, so the kernel library carries a "tune-cpu"
+    // that code from SPIR-V or the in-process front-end lacks. AArch64 counts
+    // the tuned processor family among the subtarget features it compares,
+    // so the inliner rejects such calls even for alwaysinline callees, which
+    // leaves the barrier inside a library call that the work-group passes do
+    // not see.
+    F.removeFnAttr("tune-cpu");
     // Under -cl-fast-relaxed-math the x86 backend lowers vector float
     // division to rcpps plus one Newton-Raphson step. With FMA the result
     // is within 1 ulp; without it (the x86-64 baseline, and every distro
